    "result_text": "The build is green. Here's what I implemented to satisfy the specification:\n\n## Summary\n\nThe change rejects owner creation once **100 or more** owners already carry today's `registrationDate`, responding with **HTTP 429**.\n\n**Three changes**, following the existing patterns already used for `CityAtCapacityException`, duplicate-telephone, etc.:\n\n1. **`DailyOwnerLimitException`** (new, in `rest/advice/`) \u2014 a `RuntimeException` carrying the offending day, mirroring `CityAtCapacityException`.\n\n2. **`ExceptionControllerAdvice`** \u2014 added an `@ExceptionHandler` that maps `DailyOwnerLimitException` to `HttpStatus.TOO_MANY_REQUESTS` (429) with a `ProblemDetail` body naming the `registrationDate` field, consistent with the other handlers.\n\n3. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 added a `rejectWhenDailyLimitReached()` guard (called right after field validation) plus a `DAILY_OWNER_LIMIT = 100` constant. It counts existing owners whose `registrationDate` equals `LocalDate.now()` and throws when the count reaches 100. `LocalDate.now()` matches the default registration date assigned to new owners, so the count is consistent.\n\nThe acceptance test `coreRejectsOverDailyLimit` (creates 100 owners, expects the 101st to get 429) passes: **Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0 \u2014 BUILD SUCCESS**.\n\nNote: the seed owners have a null `registration_date`, so they don't count toward the daily limit \u2014 exactly 100 same-day creates are needed to trip the rule, as the test expects.",
